        "math_academy_id": "q-68176",
        "problem": "Find the vertical asymptotes of $f(x)=\\frac{1}{4x}-\\frac{1}{x+5}.$",
        "worked_solution": "First, let's put the two terms over a common denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4x}-\\frac{1}{x+5} & =\\frac{x+5-4x}{4x(x+5)} \\\\  & =\\frac{5-3x}{4x(x+5)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator, so we proceed to set the denominator equal to zero and get\n\n$$\n4x(x+5)=0\\,\u27f9\\,x=0\\,,\\,x=-5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vertical asymptotes are at $x=0$ and $x=-5.$",
        "actual_result": "Correct",
